---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../data/site';
import ProblemFindingStory from '../components/about/ProblemFindingStory.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Favour Diokpo"
  description="How Favour finds the real problem, defines product behaviour, and designs AI systems people can trust."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-6 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        I turn messy product problems into systems people can actually trust.
      </h1>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          I am a Senior UX Specialist at Ubisoft, and I am building HERA OS.
          The work I enjoy most usually starts before the screen: figuring out
          what is actually broken, what the system should own, what the AI should
          decide, and where a human still needs to stay in control.
        </p>
        <p>
          I do not think good AI product design is about adding a smarter model
          to an old workflow. I think it is about deciding what has to be true
          around that model so a person can trust the work without carrying the
          whole system in their head.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ProblemFindingStory />

  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">02</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you decide what the AI should do — and what the product should enforce?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I separate three things that AI products often blur together:
          <strong>software rules, AI judgment, and human judgment.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          If a rule can be guaranteed cheaply in code, I do not want to spend model intelligence
          hoping the AI remembers it. If the answer requires interpretation, research, or synthesis,
          that is where the model is useful. If two valid answers depend on what the human actually
          wants, the product should come back to the human.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow">Software rules</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Things that should not depend on the AI "remembering"
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>Which piece of work is active.</li>
            <li>What the AI is allowed to access.</li>
            <li>Whether an answer has already been submitted.</li>
            <li>Whether the current result is still waiting for review.</li>
            <li>How a known capability is found and loaded.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow">AI judgment</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Things that genuinely benefit from intelligence
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>Which context is relevant to inspect.</li>
            <li>What needs research before work can continue.</li>
            <li>How to break a large outcome into bounded work.</li>
            <li>Which specialist or tool is appropriate for the job.</li>
            <li>Whether a low-risk assumption is safe enough to make.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow">Human judgment</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Things the system should never quietly invent
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>What outcome actually matters.</li>
            <li>Preferences that materially change the result.</li>
            <li>Whether a risky action is allowed.</li>
            <li>Whether a result is acceptable.</li>
            <li>When the direction itself needs to change.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">03</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you know an AI system actually works?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[52rem]">
        <p>
          I do not count an AI system as working because the answer looks good.
          I try to find the case where it can look competent while crossing a boundary it was never supposed to cross.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.92fr_1.08fr] items-start">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The failure</p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink leading-snug">
            HERA produced plausible wholesale prices by quietly making a business decision the owner had not made.
          </p>

          <div class="prose-hera mt-5">
            <p>
              The business was still deciding between fewer, higher-value stockists with stronger margins
              and broader café distribution at a thinner margin. Iris had enough information to generate
              convincing prices, so it chose a strategy itself and buried that choice inside finished-looking work.
            </p>
            <p>
              That was more dangerous than an obviously bad answer. The result looked competent enough to approve.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">What changed</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
              Assignment is execution authority, not decision authority.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-muted leading-relaxed">
              I stopped treating "is the model uncertain?" as the boundary. HERA now checks what the result materially
              depends on, who owns that decision, and whether the system is actually authorized to resolve it before
              ordinary generation continues.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-8">
            <p class="eyebrow">How I proved it</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-muted leading-relaxed">
              I tested the same architecture in two deliberately different domains: wholesale pricing and B2B studio
              positioning. In both clean real-product journeys, HERA surfaced one governing human decision instead of
              interrogating the user about every downstream consequence, while harmless implementation choices stayed autonomous.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted">
              <span class="about-proof-chip">1 human-owned root question per task</span>
              <span class="about-proof-chip">0 duplicate consequence questions</span>
              <span class="about-proof-chip">Same run resumed after the answer</span>
              <span class="about-proof-chip">Restart preserved decision identity</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line about-ai-work">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <div class="about-number-plain" aria-label="Section 04">
        <span class="about-number-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>04</span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you actually use AI in your own work?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[54rem]">
        <p>
          I do not use AI to outsource product judgment. I use it to compress the work around that judgment.
        </p>
        <p>
          My goal is to make the instruction smaller because the system around the instruction is better.
          In the extreme, I want to be able to say something as simple as "build the About page" and have the
          surrounding system already know the relevant decisions, context, permissions, workflow, acceptance
          criteria and review rules.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2 about-ai-split">
        <div class="about-ai-column">
          <p class="eyebrow">What stays with me</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Product judgment</h3>

          <ul class="mt-7 space-y-4 text-muted">
            <li><strong class="text-ink">Find</strong> the real problem.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-ink">Decide</strong> the outcome worth pursuing.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-ink">Define</strong> how the system should behave.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-ink">Choose</strong> what must remain human.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-ink">Make</strong> the important trade-offs.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-ink">Accept or reject</strong> the final result.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="about-ai-column">
          <p class="eyebrow">What I let AI accelerate</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The work around the judgment</h3>

          <ul class="mt-7 space-y-4 text-muted">
            <li>Repository and evidence research.</li>
            <li>Exploring multiple approaches quickly.</li>
            <li>Implementation from a bounded specification.</li>
            <li>Adversarial critique of a proposed solution.</li>
            <li>Independent verification.</li>
            <li>Repetitive execution and documentation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-12 max-w-[54rem]">
        <p>
          The surprising thing I learned is that getting an agent to build is often the easy part.
          The difficult part is everything that has to be true <em>before</em> you press Send:
          the context, the rules, the handoffs, the permissions, the evidence, and the definition of done.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">05</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Is this way of thinking specific to AI?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[52rem]">
        <p>
          No. HERA made the pattern easier to name, but I had been asking versions of the same systems questions
          inside enterprise products for years.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
        <article class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-[0.34fr_0.66fr]">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Ubisoft</p>
            <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
              Staffing &amp; resource planning platform
            </h3>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-base text-muted leading-relaxed">
              Producers used the tool to understand who was staffed on which project, when people were joining or
              leaving, and where future resource needs existed. The important design problem was not one screen:
              it was making a changing staffing system understandable enough that different roles could act on the same reality.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-ink">Same systems question:</strong>
              What burden is the person carrying because the product itself has not made the workflow and state clear?
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-[0.34fr_0.66fr]">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Ubisoft</p>
            <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
              Company-wide remote-work approval platform
            </h3>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-base text-muted leading-relaxed">
              What looked like an approval-form problem was really a decision-rights problem across employees,
              managers, directors, HR and governance. I translated that ambiguity into five explicit approval flows
              with clearer accountability and decision messaging under HR and legal constraints.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-ink">Same systems question:</strong>
              Who owns this decision, what should the product enforce, and what must remain human?
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-[0.34fr_0.66fr]">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Ubisoft</p>
            <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
              Hiring capacity decision support
            </h3>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-base text-muted leading-relaxed">
              The obvious product response to capacity limits was to hard-block decisions once a threshold was reached.
              I chose warning-based decision support instead, so the system could surface risk and policy limits without
              pretending software should make the staffing decision for the person.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
              <strong class="text-ink">Same systems question:</strong>
              When should software enforce a boundary, and when should it inform a human decision?
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-12 max-w-[58rem]">
        <p>
          The products changed. The questions did not:
          <strong>where should responsibility live, what should the system know, and when should it act, warn, or ask?</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .about-ai-work {
      background: #000;
    }

    .about-number-plain {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.65rem;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.76rem;
      font-weight: 650;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      border: 0;
      outline: 0;
      box-shadow: none;
      background: transparent;
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .about-number-dot {
      width: 0.38rem;
      height: 0.38rem;
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: #6697ff;
      flex: none;
    }

    .about-ai-column ul {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    .about-proof-chip {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      min-height: 2rem;
      padding: 0.4rem 0.7rem;
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--surface) 86%, transparent);
    }

    @media (max-width: 760px) {
      .about-proof-chip {
        border-radius: 0.75rem;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">What I want to work on next</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Products where behaviour is the hard part.
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I am most interested in 0→1 AI products, agentic systems, human–AI workflows,
          and complex software where trust, context, autonomy and control matter more
          than adding another screen.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line pb-20">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Explore the work</h2>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <a href={withBase('work/hera/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">HERA OS</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">See the product &rarr;</span>
        </a>


        <a href={withBase('work/lineage/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">Lineage</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">See the progression &rarr;</span>
        </a>

        <a href={withBase('contact/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">Get in touch</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">Contact &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
